            "result_text": "Here's how to test the regex pattern:\n\n```bash\n# Install Python if needed, then run:\npython3 -c \"\nimport re\n\nwith open('/app/regex.txt') as f:\n    pattern = f.read().strip()\n\nwith open('/app/sample_log.txt') as f:\n    log_text = f.read()\n\nmatches = re.findall(pattern, log_text, re.MULTILINE)\nprint('Matches found:', matches)\nfor match in matches:\n    print(f'  - {match}')\n\"\n```\n\n## Regex Pattern Explanation\n\nThe pattern I created has these components:\n\n1. **IPv4 Address Detection** (with alphanumeric boundaries):\n   - `(?<![A-Za-z0-9])` - Not preceded by alphanumeric\n   - `(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1[0-9]{2}|[1-9]?[0-9])\\.(...)`  - Matches each octet (0-255 without leading zeros)\n   - `(?![A-Za-z0-9])` - Not followed by alphanumeric\n\n2. **Date Capture** (last date only):\n   - `(\\d{4}-...)` - Captures the date in YYYY-MM-DD format\n   - Date validation by month:\n     - **31-day months** (01,03,05,07,08,10,12): days 01-31\n     - **30-day months** (04,06,09,11): days 01-30  \n     - **February** (02): days 01-29 (as specified)\n\n3. **Last Date Enforcement**:\n   - `(?!.*date_pattern)` - Negative lookahead ensures no more valid dates follow on the same line\n\n4. **Boundary Checks**:\n   - Uses `(?<![A-Za-z0-9])` and `(?![A-Za-z0-9])` to prevent false matches with dates like \"user1134-12-1234\"\n\nThe regex correctly handles edge cases like leading zeros in IPv4 octets, invalid dates (e.g., Feb 30, April 31), and false matches with alphanumeric-adjacent patterns."
